    "result_text": "The build succeeds and all tests pass (`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and its `coreRejectsOverDailyLimit` now passes).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-creation limit rule following the OfficeFloor function-injection conventions in this repo, closely mirroring the existing `EnsureCityCapacity` / `CityAtCapacityException` pattern.\n\n**New files:**\n- `rest/function/owner/EnsureDailyOwnerLimit.java` \u2014 a pipeline step that counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals today and throws `DailyOwnerLimitException` when the count is \u2265 100. Runs before `BuildOwner`/`SaveOwner`, so the incoming owner isn't yet counted (100 existing \u2192 the 101st is rejected).\n- `rest/escalation/DailyOwnerLimitException.java` \u2014 checked exception carrying the date/count, with `LIMIT = 100`.\n- `rest/escalation/DailyOwnerLimitExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `429 TOO_MANY_REQUESTS`.\n- `officefloor/escalation/\u2026DailyOwnerLimitException.yml` \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n**Edited:**\n- `officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted an `ensureDailyLimit` step (governed by `transaction`) between `ensureCityCapacity` and `build`.\n\nThe rule counts by `registrationDate` (the created-date field, defaulted to `LocalDate.now()` in `BuildOwner`), matching the spec, and returns 429 on rejection.",
